NODE_ENV=development
PORT=4000
APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3007

# Browser-reachable URL of **this API** (no trailing slash).
# REQUIRED on Render/Vercel: set to your public HTTPS API host (e.g. https://aso-trmg.onrender.com).
# Upload responses and cached localhost URLs in JSON are rewritten to this origin when producing API responses (see publicMediaUrl).
# Development: http://localhost:4000 (match PORT). Production: unset only if uploads + image URLs resolve correctly without it (not typical on Render).
# API_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://localhost:4000

# Optional — store uploads on Cloudinary (recommended on Render; all three required to enable).
# CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
# CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=
# CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=

# Local: mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/SHOPIQGH  |  Atlas: use your SRV string; add your IP under Atlas → Network Access.
# Login/refresh return 503 if this host is unreachable (firewall, wrong IP allow list, or MongoDB not running).
# Optional: one-off URL migration script `node fixUrls.js` uses:
# REPLACE_UPLOAD_URL_FROM=http://localhost:4000
# REPLACE_UPLOAD_URL_TO=https://your-api.onrender.com
# Optional: `node migrateImages.js` uploads local backend/uploads files to Cloudinary and updates DB (needs CLOUDINARY_*).
# Set MIGRATE_IMAGES_DRY_RUN=1 to log only. MIGRATE_IMAGES_SKIP=products,users skips sections.
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/SHOPIQGH

JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=replace-with-at-least-32-random-characters
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=replace-with-another-32-random-characters

COOKIE_SECURE=false

# ---- Email ----
# For Gmail, set EMAIL_FROM to the same account, e.g. SHOPIQGH <you@gmail.com>
EMAIL_FROM=SHOPIQGH <no-reply@example.com>

# (C) Brevo — recommended on Render *free* tier (SMTP ports 587/465 blocked). https://www.brevo.com
# Create API key (SMTP & API → API keys). Add + verify your Gmail under Senders (OTP). No domain purchase needed.
# BREVO_API_KEY=xkeysib-...
# EMAIL_FROM=SHOPIQGH <you@gmail.com>
# BREVO_SENDER_EMAIL=
# BREVO_SENDER_NAME=

# (A) Gmail SMTP (local dev): 2-Step Verification + App password, leave SMTP_HOST empty
# EMAIL_USER=you@gmail.com
# EMAIL_PASS=xxxx xxxx xxxx xxxx

# (B) SMTP (works locally; blocked on Render free unless you use Brevo or paid Render)
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMS_PROVIDER=none
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=

STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Card / MoMo checkout via Paystack (Ghana GHS). When set, the API routes buyer checkout and refunds through Paystack only:
# Register webhook (charges + refund status):
# POST https://<your-api-host>/api/payments/paystack/webhook
PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY=

# When true, after a successful card/MoMo Paystack charge the API automatically sends each vendor their
# `sellerProceeds` via Paystack Transfers (from your Paystack balance). You must enable Transfers in the
# Paystack Dashboard; each seller must register `POST /api/vendor/paystack/payout-account` (after listing banks
# via `GET /api/vendor/paystack/ghana-banks`). The platform’s commission stays in your Paystack balance minus
# those transfers and Paystack’s own fees. Ignored for orders paid with checkout split (see PAYSTACK_CHECKOUT_SPLIT).
PAYSTACK_AUTO_VENDOR_PAYOUT=false

# When not `false`, Paystack /transaction/initialize uses split payment: each seller’s sellerProceeds (pesewas) go to
# their Paystack subaccount (created when they save payout details). Remainder stays on the main merchant account
# (platform fee + processing margin). Set PAYSTACK_CHECKOUT_SPLIT=false to use the legacy “full charge + optional transfers” model.
# PAYSTACK_CHECKOUT_SPLIT=false

# Platform service fee (% of seller’s list price), added on top for the buyer at checkout.
# Example: seller price Ghc 50, PLATFORM_COMMISSION_PERCENT=5 → Ghc 2.50 service fee; Paystack total solves so the platform receives list + fee after gateway charges.
PLATFORM_COMMISSION_PERCENT=5

# Seller platform subscription: free for VENDOR_TRIAL_MONTHS after PLATFORM_DEPLOYED_AT, then annual fee.
# PLATFORM_DEPLOYED_AT=2026-05-15
# VENDOR_TRIAL_MONTHS=2
# VENDOR_SUBSCRIPTION_PRICE_GHS=49
# VENDOR_SUBSCRIPTION_PERIOD_MONTHS=12

# Linear model of Paystack’s cut on the buyer’s GHS charge (tune to your Paystack Ghana pricing).
# Buyer total T solves: T − (PAYSTACK_CHECKOUT_FEE_PERCENT/100×T + PAYSTACK_CHECKOUT_FEE_FIXED_GHS) = itemsSubtotal + serviceFees.
PAYSTACK_CHECKOUT_FEE_PERCENT=1.95
PAYSTACK_CHECKOUT_FEE_FIXED_GHS=0.10

# Local dev only: true = skip verify-email flow (register auto-verifies; login allowed)
AUTH_SKIP_EMAIL_VERIFICATION=false

# Production debugging only: true = print OTPs to server stdout (Render → Logs). OTPs still emailed; never returned in API JSON.
# Off by default — enable briefly on Render if email fails, then set back to false.
LOG_OTP_IN_CONSOLE=false

# Local demo only: true = buyers can rate products without a paid order (still one review per buyer per product).
REVIEWS_SKIP_VERIFIED_PURCHASE=false

# Optional. When set, all `/api/admin` requests must send header `X-Admin-Secret: <same value>`.
# Mirror in the frontend as `REACT_APP_ADMIN_API_KEY` if you use this.
ADMIN_ACCESS_SECRET=

# Fixed platform admin: when MongoDB is offline, these credentials still sign in (JWT-only session, no OTP possible).
# When the DB is up, this user is created/updated on API start; sign-in uses the same email OTP as everyone else (unless AUTH_SKIP_EMAIL_VERIFICATION=true).
# Use a strong BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD — never commit real values.
# Optional: stable 24-hex ObjectId for JWT subject (default used if unset). Set once if you care about stable ids in logs.
# BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_JWT_SUB=
# BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com
# BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=Adm!9kX2#mQ7vL

# Comma- or semicolon-separated extra platform “super” admin emails (can grant other users the admin role in Admin → Users).
# The `BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL` user is always super when that account exists. Sign in again after changing this so JWTs pick up `adminLevel`.
# SUPER_USER_EMAILS=founder1@example.com,founder2@example.com

# ---- Groq Cloud — optional shopping assistant (recommended on CPU-only laptops) ----
# When `GROQ_API_KEY` is set, the assistant uses Groq’s OpenAI-compatible API and **does not** call local Ollama (startup Ollama warmup is skipped).
# Free API key: https://console.groq.com (no credit card required on the free tier).
#
# GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_...
# GROQ_MODEL=llama3-8b-8192
# GROQ_MAX_TOKENS=300
# Optional: HTTP timeout for non-streaming Groq calls (ms). When unset/0, uses OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_MS below.
# GROQ_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
#
# Shopping assistant abuse protection (POST /api/assistant/chat — per IP, separate from the global API limit).
# Default window: 15 minutes. When ASSISTANT_CHAT_RATE_LIMIT_MAX is 0/unset: 45 requests in production, 400 in development.
# ASSISTANT_CHAT_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=900000
# ASSISTANT_CHAT_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=0
#
# ---- Local AI (Ollama) — optional shopping assistant (used when Groq is unset) ----
# Point at the machine running Ollama (default port 11434). If you see "model … not found", run `ollama pull <model>` then match OLLAMA_MODEL to `ollama list`.

# Default model in code is **llama3.2:3b** (`ollama pull llama3.2:3b`) — stronger instruction-following than 1b for category boundaries and links.
# For very slow CPUs you can try **llama3.2:1b** or **phi3:mini** at the cost of more mistakes.
# Large models (mistral:latest, llama3:70b-instruct‑q*) on CPU are slow; timeouts may still feel “instant” vs wall‑clock latency.
# If each answer takes **5–10+ minutes**, switch to a smaller quant (or add a GPU); app defaults favor balanced quality on typical dev machines.
#
# Avoid `OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_MS=60000` unless you accept frequent timeouts — first CPU runs after `ollama pull` can be slow.
#
# Copy/tune below (unset = code defaults: llama3.2:3b, predict 200, ctx 1024, temp 0.3, thread 8):
#
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434
# OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.2:3b
# OLLAMA_NUM_PREDICT=200
# OLLAMA_NUM_CTX=1024
# OLLAMA_NUM_THREAD=8
# OLLAMA_TEMPERATURE=0.3
# OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_MS=300000
#
# If chats time out on cold model pulls only, raise briefly (max 900000):
# OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
